---
import { asset } from '../lib/asset';

const causes = [
  {
    title: 'Near-duplicate frames',
    body: 'When the scene changes slowly, consecutive frames add almost nothing, yet each one still takes its turn in the queue ahead of the next informative frame.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Low information density',
    body: 'Sky, road surface and distant background fill most of each frame or scan. They cost compute and memory without changing the next decision.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Vibration',
    body: 'Motion blur in the camera and ghost points and distortion in LiDAR make an unchanged scene look different, so similarity filters let redundant data through.',
  },
];
---

<section class="section" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="problem-h">The staleness gap</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Field failures in agentic robots often trace to a sensor-to-decision bottleneck, not a weak model. Per-frame
        accuracy and latency can look good while the robot acts on frames the scene has already left behind.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="gap-grid">
      <figure>
        <div class="figure-frame fig1">
          <img
            src={asset('images/fig1-staleness-gap.webp')}
            width="1600"
            height="794"
            loading="lazy"
            alt="Line chart of present-world accuracy for 20 detectors, M1 to M20. A blue scene-current upper bound stays between about 0.75 and 0.98; a red default full-rate FIFO line stays between about 0.28 and 0.45, an average gap of 0.44. Below, frames from t minus N to t minus 1 are processed while the current scene at time t shows a pedestrian and a scooter the stale result misses."
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="caption">
          Paper, Fig. 1. Replaying the same streams through the default full-rate FIFO pipeline (red) against a
          scene-current upper bound (blue) across 20 widely used detectors. The FIFO output trails by 0.44
          present-world accuracy on average, because the queue keeps inference on frames from t − N to t − 1 while the
          scene has moved to t.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="why">
        <h3>Where the lag comes from</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Low-value inputs reach the pipeline at full rate and wait in line with everything else. Three kinds dominate:
        </p>
        <dl>
          {
            causes.map((c) => (
              <div>
                <dt>{c.title}</dt>
                <dd>{c.body}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="skew">
      <h3>The same data skew learning</h3>
      <p>
        Over longer time scales, routine logging fills with frequent, low-signal scenes, while the rare, difficult
        events that shape the decision boundary stay under-sampled. More data becomes more of the same, and robustness
        to rare conditions stalls.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .gap-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.45fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 64px);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .fig1 {
    padding: clamp(0.5rem, 1.5vw, 1rem);
  }

  .why h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  dl {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  dl > div {
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--stale);
  }

  dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }

  dd {
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .skew {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 18rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem 3rem;
    margin-top: clamp(48px, 6vw, 72px);
    padding-top: 1.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .skew p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .gap-grid,
    .skew {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
